Here's a modular Perl script that checks if a string is a palindrome. It ignores whitespace and punctuation, handles any length, validates input, and outputs a clear result.

```perl
#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
use utf8;
use Text::Unidecode;   # handles non-ASCII characters (optional)

# Check if Text::Unidecode is available; fall back gracefully if not
eval "require Text::Unidecode";
my $has_unidecode = not $@;

# Main program
my $input = get_input();

if (not defined $input) {
    print "No input provided. Please pass a string as a command-line argument.\n";
    exit 1;
}

my $clean = normalize($input);

if (is_palindrome($clean)) {
    print "\"$input\" is a palindrome (ignoring whitespace and punctuation).\n";
} else {
    print "\"$input\" is not a palindrome.\n";
}

exit 0;

#==============================================================================
# get_input: read from @ARGV or from standard input
#==============================================================================
sub get_input {
    if (@ARGV) {
        return join(' ', @ARGV);
    }

    # No arguments: read one line from STDIN
    local $/ = "\n";    # slurp one line
    my $line = <STDIN>;
    return defined $line ? $line : undef;
}

#==============================================================================
# normalize: strip whitespace and punctuation, lowercase the string
#==============================================================================
sub normalize {
    my ($str) = @_;
    $str //= '';

    # Remove leading/trailing whitespace
    $str =~ s/^\s+|\s+$//g;

    # Remove everything that is not a letter or a digit
    $str =~ s/[^A-Za-z0-9]//g;

    # Lowercase for case-insensitive comparison
    $str = lc($str);

    # Try to transliterate non-ASCII letters to ASCII (e.g., é -> e)
    if ($has_unidecode) {
        $str = Text::Unidecode::unidecode($str);
    }

    return $str;
}

#==============================================================================
# is_palindrome: return true if the string reads the same forwards and backwards
#==============================================================================
sub is_palindrome {
    my ($str) = @_;
    return 0 unless length $str;   # empty string is not treated as a palindrome

    my $len = length $str;
    for my $i (0 .. int($len / 2) - 1) {
        my $j = $len - 1 - $i;
        return 0 if substr($str, $i, 1) ne substr($str, $j, 1);
    }
    return 1;
}

__END__

=head1 NAME

palindrome.pl - Check whether a string is a palindrome

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  perl palindrome.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
  echo "Race car!" | perl palindrome.pl

=head1 DESCRIPTION

This script checks if a given string is a palindrome, ignoring whitespace,
punctuation, and letter case. It also transliterates non-ASCII letters to ASCII
when the Text::Unidecode module is available.

An empty string or a string with no letters or digits is not considered a
palindrome.

=head1 USAGE

  perl palindrome.pl "YOUR STRING HERE"

Or pipe a line to it:

  echo "YOUR STRING HERE" | perl palindrome.pl

=head1 REQUIRES

  perl
  Text::Unidecode (optional, for non-ASCII characters)

=cut
```

How it works:

- `get_input` reads from command-line arguments, or from standard input if nothing is passed.
- `normalize` strips whitespace and punctuation, lowercases the string, and transliterates non-ASCII characters when possible.
- `is_palindrome` compares characters from both ends moving toward the middle.
- The script exits with status 0 on success and 1 if no input was given.

To run it:

```sh
perl palindrome.pl "A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"
perl palindrome.pl "Hello, world!"
echo "Race car!" | perl palindrome.pl
```

If you'd rather not install Text::Unidecode, the script still works. It will just keep non-ASCII letters as they are, so "évé" would be compared as "évé" rather than "eve".

Here's a program that does what you asked for. It reads the string and the pattern from the user, compiles the pattern, counts the matching lines, and handles bad input with clear messages.

```java
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.util.regex.Matcher;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);

        System.out.print("Enter a string (multiline input is fine): ");
        String text = scanner.nextLine();

        System.out.print("Enter a regular expression: ");
        String patternInput = scanner.nextLine();

        try {
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(patternInput);

            int count = countMatchingLines(text, pattern);

            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Result:");
            System.out.println("  Lines that match:  " + count);

        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println();
            System.out.println("Error: the regular expression is invalid.");
            System.out.println("  Pattern: " + patternInput);
            System.out.println("  Message: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        scanner.close();
    }

    /**
     * Counts the lines in {@code text} that match {@code pattern}.
     * An empty {@code text} has zero matching lines.
     */
    private static int countMatchingLines(String text, Pattern pattern) {
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int count = 0;
        String[] lines = text.split("\n", -1);

        for (String line : lines) {
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

How it works:

- It reads the whole string first, including newlines, then asks for the regular expression. If you ask for the pattern first, you have to type the string in one line, which is easy to get wrong.
- It compiles the pattern inside a try-catch. If the pattern is bad, it prints the pattern and the error message, and doesn't try to count.
- It splits the input on `\n` and checks each line with `matcher.find()`. That means it counts a line if the pattern matches anywhere in it. If you want to count only lines that match the whole string, use `matcher.matches()` instead.
- It passes `-1` to `split("\n", -1)`. Without that, a string that ends with a newline loses its last empty line. With it, `"a\nb\n"` gives three lines: `"a"`, `"b"`, and `""`. Change that to `split("\n")` if you'd rather drop trailing empty lines.
- The empty-string case is handled in `countMatchingLines`. An empty string has no lines to match, so the count is 0.

Example run:

```
Enter a string (multiline input is fine): hello
Enter a regular expression: [0-9]+
Error: the regular expression is invalid.
  Pattern: [0-9]+
  Message: Unclosed character class near index 1
   [0-9]+
   ^
```

Another run, with a valid pattern:

```
Enter a string (multiline input is fine): apple
Enter a regular expression: p
Result:
  Lines that match:  1
```

If you'd rather put the string on the command line, you can do that too. Then the program looks like this:

```java
import java.util.regex.Pattern;
import java.util.regex.PatternSyntaxException;

public class LineCounter {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length < 2) {
            System.out.println("Usage: java LineCounter <string> <regex>");
            System.out.println("Example: java LineCounter \"hello\" \"[0-9]+\"");
            return;
        }

        String text = args[0];
        String patternInput = args[1];

        try {
            Pattern pattern = Pattern.compile(patternInput);
            int count = countMatchingLines(text, pattern);
            System.out.println("Lines that match: " + count);
        } catch (PatternSyntaxException e) {
            System.out.println("Error: invalid regular expression");
            System.out.println("  Pattern: " + patternInput);
            System.out.println("  Message: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    private static int countMatchingLines(String text, Pattern pattern) {
        if (text.isEmpty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int count = 0;
        for (String line : text.split("\n", -1)) {
            if (pattern.matcher(line).find()) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

Run it like this:

```
java LineCounter "hello" "[0-9]+"
```

When you put a string on the command line, the shell takes care of most quoting, but if your pattern has spaces or special characters, wrap it in quotes. The same goes for the string if it has spaces.

A custom loading spinner is a small animation that appears while content is being fetched. It sits where the content will, or overlays it, and usually keeps the page's layout from jumping. Here are its advantages in the three areas you named.

Visual design
- It carries your brand. A spinner coloured and shaped to match your identity looks like part of the product, whereas a browser's default turnstile looks like the browser.
- It hides unfinished work. A plain text block or a broken image frame draws the eye to what's missing. A spinner tells the eye that something is happening and will be here soon.
- It keeps the layout stable. Placeholders that reserve space prevent content from jumping as images and widgets load. That also helps with Core Web Vitals, and LCP is part of what Google ranks by.
- It sets the right mood. A calm, measured animation suggests the app is working. A frantic one suggests it is struggling.

Loading time
- It is light. A spinner is usually a few kilobytes of SVG or a CSS-only animation, so it adds almost nothing to the load.
- It does not delay anything. Unlike an overlay that waits for everything to finish, a spinner can start as soon as the first request goes out.
- It makes the wait feel shorter. People judge a wait partly by how much they see of it. A visible, purposeful animation makes a few seconds feel shorter than the same seconds with nothing on screen.
- It can show progress. A progress bar or a percentage, or a skeleton screen that fills in, lets people estimate how long is left. That is especially useful on slow connections.

Interactive experience
- It answers the user. The moment someone clicks, the interface says "I got that, it is loading." Without it, people assume nothing happened and click again, or leave.
- It prevents bad actions. A disabled button or a "please wait" state stops someone from submitting a form twice or navigating away mid-action.
- It carries error messages. If a request fails, the same spot can show a retry button, so the user does not have to guess what went wrong.
- It can give a micro-interaction. A small pulse or a change of colour when something finishes can confirm an action without taking the user out of the flow.

A few practical points
- Keep it short. Most people tolerate about two seconds before they start to worry. If it will take longer, show progress or tell them to come back later.
- Match the pace to the task. A quick local action needs a quick spinner; a large file upload needs a progress bar.
- Make it accessible. Use an aria-live region or a visible label, and make sure the animation does not trigger the user's motion preferences (respects prefers-reduced-motion).
- Don't overdo it. If every small hover has its own spinner, the page will feel nervous.

A simple CSS-only spinner is enough for most cases:

```html
<div class="loader" role="status" aria-label="Loading"></div>
```

```css
.loader {
  display: inline-block;
  width: 40px;
  height: 40px;
  border: 3px solid #f0f0f0;
  border-top-color: #3b82f6;
  border-radius: 50%;
  animation: spin 360ms linear infinite;
}

@keyframes spin {
  from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
  to   { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
```

Replace the top colour with your brand colour, and add a message below it if the wait may run over a couple of seconds.
